---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EmergenceHeader from '../../components/EmergenceHeader.astro';
import EmergenceFooter from '../../components/EmergenceFooter.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Ethical Hardware Enclave — Sovereign Compute | The Emergence Institute"
  description="The Ethical Hardware Enclave: localized AI inference, hardware-level security, and anti-monopoly infrastructure. Sovereign compute that keeps your data in your possession."
>
  <EmergenceHeader slot="header" />

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="label gold">Sovereign Infrastructure</p>
      <h1>The Ethical Hardware Enclave</h1>
      <p class="sub"><em>Software protocols are not enough. The silicon must be sovereign too.</em></p>
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p class="intro">
        To achieve true cognitive sovereignty and data ownership, the underlying physical
        infrastructure must be secured. The Ethical Hardware Enclave is the Emergence
        Collective's commitment to <strong>localized, self-hosted execution</strong> —
        running premium AI models and cryptographic protocols on hardware you control,
        not on someone else's computer. No cloud dependency. No data harvesting. No
        remote kill switch.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THREE PILLARS -->
  <section class="section section--dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p class="label gold">Three Pillars</p>
      <h2>What makes hardware ethical?</h2>
      <p class="muted layer-intro">
        An "ethical" hardware deployment isn't about the brand of the silicon. It's
        about <em>who controls it</em>, <em>where data flows</em>, and <em>whether
        anyone can pull the plug</em>. These three pillars define the standard.
      </p>

      <div class="layer-stack">
        <div class="layer-card">
          <p class="layer-num gold">Pillar I</p>
          <h3>Localized Inference <span class="muted">· Sovereign Compute</span></h3>
          <p>
            Premium AI models run on <strong>local, dedicated hardware workstations</strong>
            — multi-GPU setups running Linux, homelab servers, or single-board
            computers for lightweight inference. Data never leaves the node's physical
            possession. When you query a model, the prompt and the response stay on
            your machine. No telemetry. No training on your data. No third-party API
            that can log, rate-limit, or revoke access. <em>Absolute privacy by
            physical architecture.</em>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="layer-card">
          <p class="layer-num gold">Pillar II</p>
          <h3>Hardware-Level Security <span class="muted">· Trusted Execution</span></h3>
          <p>
            The "Enclave" is literal: sensitive cryptographic operations, model
            execution, and community data processing run inside <strong>hardware-isolated
            environments</strong>. TPM 2.0 anchors node identity so keys can never be
            silently exfiltrated or impersonated. Secure enclaves (Intel SGX / AMD
            SEV) sandbox computation so that even a compromised host OS cannot inspect
            the data being processed. What happens in the enclave stays in the enclave.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="layer-card">
          <p class="layer-num gold">Pillar III</p>
          <h3>Anti-Monopoly Infrastructure <span class="muted">· Distributed Resilience</span></h3>
          <p>
            By distributing compute across an <strong>independent mesh of local
            nodes</strong> rather than a single cloud provider, the Ethical Hardware
            Enclave ensures the network is resilient against hardware-level shutoffs,
            corporate rentier capitalism, and external systemic shocks. No single
            entity owns enough nodes to halt the network. No data center migration
            can silently capture a community's compute. The digital collective is
            grounded in <em>physical, self-reliant reality</em> — not in a terms-of-service
            agreement that can change overnight.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- NODE TIERS -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p class="label gold">Hardware Tiers</p>
      <h2>From Raspberry Pi to multi-GPU workstation</h2>
      <p class="intro">
        The Enclave is not one-size-fits-all. Nodes scale from lightweight edge
        devices that verify and relay, to high-compute synthesis nodes that run
        large language models locally. Every tier meets the three-pillar standard.
      </p>

      <div class="tier-grid">
        <div class="tier-card">
          <p class="tier-num gold">Tier 1</p>
          <h3>Edge Node</h3>
          <p class="tier-hw">Raspberry Pi 5 · 8 GB RAM</p>
          <p>
            Verifies trilateral receipts, relays mesh traffic, and participates in
            lightweight consensus. Holds a TPM-anchored identity. Cannot run large
            models, but can verify that others did. The smallest sovereign unit.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tier-card">
          <p class="tier-num gold">Tier 2</p>
          <h3>Standard Node</h3>
          <p class="tier-hw">Homelab server · 32+ GB RAM · Consumer GPU</p>
          <p>
            Runs mid-size open-weight models locally (7B–13B parameters). Handles
            routine synthesis work, Georgist ledger computation, and community data
            processing. The backbone of the mesh.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tier-card">
          <p class="tier-num gold">Tier 3</p>
          <h3>Synthesis Node</h3>
          <p class="tier-hw">Multi-GPU workstation · 64+ GB RAM · Ubuntu/Linux</p>
          <p>
            Runs large models (70B+) entirely on local hardware. Handles complex
            adversarial synthesis rounds, multi-agent orchestration, and high-stakes
            governance proposals that require deep reasoning. The engine room.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECURITY MODEL -->
  <section class="section section--dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p class="label gold">Security Model</p>
      <h2>Defense in depth, from silicon to mesh</h2>

      <div class="spec-grid">
        <div class="spec-card">
          <h3>TPM 2.0 Identity Anchoring</h3>
          <p>
            Every node's Ed25519 keypair is bound to a TPM 2.0 chip. The private key
            never leaves the hardware security module. A node's identity cannot be
            copied, exfiltrated, or silently impersonated — even with root access to
            the operating system.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="spec-card">
          <h3>Secure Enclave Execution</h3>
          <p>
            Sensitive computation — model inference on personal data, cryptographic
            signing, consensus verification — runs inside hardware-enforced trusted
            execution environments (SGX/SEV). The host OS cannot inspect enclave
            memory. A compromised kernel cannot read your queries.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="spec-card">
          <h3>Air-Gapped Key Management</h3>
          <p>
            Long-lived signing keys for governance receipts are generated and stored
            on air-gapped hardware. They sign trilateral receipts through a
            one-way data diode — the signing device receives the hash, signs it, and
            returns only the signature. The key never touches a networked system.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="spec-card">
          <h3>Mesh-Level Verification</h3>
          <p>
            Even if a single node is compromised, the mesh architecture limits the
            blast radius. Trilateral receipts require three independent signatures.
            Adversarial consensus rounds catch anomalous behavior. The network is
            designed to degrade gracefully, not fail catastrophically.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HOW IT INTERSECTS -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p class="label gold">The Intersection</p>
      <h2>Governance meets hardware</h2>
      <p class="intro">
        The <strong>Tripartite Council</strong> provides the cognitive and organizational
        framework for decentralized governance. The <strong>Ethical Hardware
        Enclave</strong> provides the physical, sovereign soil upon which that
        framework runs safely. One without the other is incomplete: governance
        without sovereign hardware is a terms-of-service agreement. Hardware without
        governance is just a computer. Together, they ensure the Emergence Collective
        remains self-sovereign, secure, and structurally aligned with its foundational
        principles — from the silicon up.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CROSS-LINKS -->
  <section class="section section--dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p class="label gold">Continue Reading</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href="/emergence/tripartite-council" class="btn">The Tripartite Council →</a>
        <a href="/emergence/architecture" class="btn">Lattice Architecture →</a>
        <a href="/emergence/lattice-spec" class="btn">Protocol Specification →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <EmergenceFooter slot="footer" />
</Base>

<style>
  .muted { color: var(--text-muted); }
  .sub { font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .intro { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; max-width: 720px; color: var(--text-muted); }
  .layer-intro { font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 2rem; max-width: 680px; }

  .layer-stack {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .layer-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }

  .layer-num {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }

  .layer-card h3 {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .layer-card p {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .tier-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .tier-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }

  .tier-num {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }

  .tier-card h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
  }

  .tier-hw {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-family: monospace;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    margin: 0.1rem 0 0.6rem;
  }

  .tier-card p:last-child {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.65;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .spec-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .spec-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }

  .spec-card h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .spec-card p {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.65;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 780px) {
    .tier-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .spec-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .cta-row { flex-direction: column; }
  }
</style>
